{
  "issue": "ELX-EXPORTS",
  "note": "`overrides` measured GAP -> implemented. `defoverridable` is Elixir's override construct and the only one the language has: it marks definitions the module already published as extension points, and a later definition of the same name/arity replaces them, reaching the previous one through `super/1`. Measured: 26 forms in 14 of 559 corpus files, 65 name/arity pairs -> 18 edges in 2 files, both endpoints resolving 18/18. The edge is from the LATER definition to the PRIOR one, so both endpoints name the same function and `overridesLine` carries the declaration `super/1` reaches (bash BD8b records the FIRST declaration for every redefinition; the chain is more truthful here). Arity is part of the identity. A mark with nothing after it is an extension POINT, not an override. The MODULE form (`defoverridable Other.Mod`, 3 corpus sites) records the mark on the symbol and publishes NO edge: the definitions it marks live in another file and 'who points at me' is a subsystem that does not exist yet — declared as a limitation, not guessed at. Falsifier: mutant elixir/overrides-mark-without-prior, elixir/overrides-arity-blind, elixir/overrides-line-first-decl. A mark that PRECEDES the declaration it names produces no edge either: there is no prior definition to override.",
